# The engine-execution layer of the SDK: twelve modules (ten leaves plus the
# plan gate modules) and the engine context with its SQL door and its plan
# door (see README.md). Each module's header says what it is for.
#
# The welded tests (each file's header says which defect it catches):
# - test_plan_cache_full_sink_hash: the sink kind is part of the Layer 2 cache
#   key, the path is not, and the key is deterministic.
# - test_resolved_cache_pin_count: a pinned entry survives an eviction wave,
#   pins are counted, and a cache whose entries are all pinned refuses an insert.
# - test_resolved_cache_lru_eviction_stress: past the cap the oldest unpinned
#   entry is evicted and the rest stay reachable.
# - test_plan_validation_routes_ledger: the route ledger's self check, token
#   lookup and status names, and the scan-binding wiring refusing an
#   unregistered token and a handle from another epoch.
# - test_plan_validation_gate_direct: the gate's outcome for a scan, a union
#   under a join, and a filter on a missing column; the ledger check on the
#   entry token; the tag names; the glob-options mapping; the switch default.
# - test_arrow_result: run_sql answers (ints, strings, NULLs, decimals, an
#   empty answer) read back value for value by the C consumer of
#   komira_test_arrow_c_consumer after the context is dropped, every buffer
#   released afterwards; refusals, a selection mask and batch order.
# - test_secret_capability_reveal: a capability reveals exactly the secret bound
#   to its own node id, once, and an unbound node or a removed ref raises before
#   the consumer is called.
# - test_run_sql_q6: run_sql over an EngineContext. TPC-H Q6's text from
#   bench_corpus over a lineitem-shaped Parquet file gives the integer
#   oracle's DECIMAL(38,4); a self-join counts the key pairs of the oracle; a
#   LEFT self-join counts the pairs and the unmatched rows of the oracle and a
#   scalar subquery gives the oracle's count; a LEFT JOIN with a residual over
#   both sides, a syntax error, an unknown table, a CTAS and a replaced file
#   are named refusals; the footer is read once per
#   query; contexts are created and dropped repeatedly.
# - test_run_sql_distinct: run_sql over Parquet tables in several row groups:
#   SELECT DISTINCT over an INT64 and a STRING column gives each value once and
#   one NULL row (NULL and the empty string are two rows), below a filter and
#   with ORDER BY (NULLs last); COUNT(DISTINCT x) ignores NULL, is an INT64 that
#   is 0 for no rows and for all NULL, counts per group (the NULL key and an
#   all-NULL group included), runs beside SUM and over a STRING; SELECT DISTINCT
#   over two INT64 columns gives each pair once.
# - test_run_sql_float_sum_workers: run_sql over a DOUBLE and a FLOAT column in
#   small row groups on 1, 2, 3 and 7 workers, several runs each: an ungrouped
#   sum() and avg() have the same bits on every worker count, equal to a model
#   that adds each row group from +0.0 in row order and the row groups in file
#   order (exact 1.0 / 0.25 for 1e16, 1, -1e16, 1; mixed magnitudes with NULLs;
#   under a WHERE), and an integer sum beside them stays an exact DECIMAL(38, 0).
# - test_run_sql_group_keys: run_sql over a Parquet table of DATE32, BOOL,
#   DECIMAL(12, 2), DECIMAL(30, 4) and INT64 columns in several row groups:
#   GROUP BY one DATE32, BOOL or DECIMAL column equals an oracle computed in
#   the test (the NULL key is one group; count(*), count(v), sum(v); decimal
#   keys past INT64 exact), min/max of dates and sum of decimals run under a
#   BOOL key, a filter before the grouping, an all-NULL key is one group, and a
#   key of a DATE32 and a BOOL column equals the oracle too.
# - test_run_sql_group_keys_mixed: run_sql over a Parquet table DuckDB wrote
#   (the :group_keys_mixed_oracle action below) answers what the pinned DuckDB
#   answers for each case of tests/group_keys_mixed/cases.sql: GROUP BY an
#   INTEGER, a VARCHAR and a DATE (both orders), a BIGINT and an INTEGER, a
#   BOOLEAN, a DECIMAL and a VARCHAR, with NULLs in every key column and the
#   empty string, under a WHERE and a HAVING, with count(DISTINCT), SELECT
#   DISTINCT over three columns, an all-NULL key and no row, on one and three
#   workers; every case is run.
# - test_run_sql_iceberg: run_sql over an Iceberg table written in the test
#   (format version 2, three columns, no snapshot): iceberg_scan of a table
#   directory and of its metadata file answers the schema and no row, under a
#   projection, a filter, an ORDER BY and count(*); allow_moved_paths is
#   accepted; a snapshot the table lacks, a directory with no version hint and
#   an s3:// path are named refusals at step bind; every other DuckDB option is
#   refused at step parse by its exact message; register_iceberg_table over a
#   StorageBasedCatalog (a bare path and a file:/// URI) is queried by name
#   without regard to case, passes allow_moved_paths, and refuses an unknown
#   table and a location off the local file system by exact message; an Iceberg
#   and a Parquet table join in either order; the kind name lowering cuts an
#   Iceberg scan by is the binding's.
# - test_run_sql_join_kinds: run_sql over Parquet tables DuckDB wrote (the
#   :join_kinds_oracle action above) answers what the pinned DuckDB answers for
#   each case of tests/join_kinds/cases.sql, on one and three workers: LEFT,
#   RIGHT and FULL joins on BIGINT, VARCHAR, INTEGER = BIGINT, DOUBLE and
#   two-column keys, under counts, filters and chained joins, and against an
#   empty table on either side; EXISTS, NOT EXISTS, IN, NOT IN (NULL-aware) and
#   the SEMI JOIN and ANTI JOIN keywords; CROSS joins (also past one output
#   chunk), a comma join and scalar subqueries; a LEFT JOIN with a residual over
#   both sides is refused at lower; every case is run.
# - test_run_sql_correlated: run_sql over eight TPC-H-typed Parquet tables
#   DuckDB wrote (the :correlated_oracle action above) answers what the pinned
#   DuckDB answers for each case of tests/correlated/cases.sql, on one and
#   three workers: the corpus texts of TPC-H Q4, Q17, Q20 and Q22, and Q2's
#   body without its ORDER BY and LIMIT; correlated scalars over no row, with
#   NULL keys, two keys, in a WHERE anywhere (BETWEEN too), in a SELECT list
#   and a derived table; EXISTS and NOT EXISTS with NULL keys; Q21, Q2 (whose
#   ORDER BY is a DECIMAL key) and the shapes it does not serve are refused
#   by name at their step; the TPC-H cases are the corpus texts; every case
#   is run.
# - test_run_sql_subquery: run_sql over Parquet tables DuckDB wrote (the
#   :subquery_oracle action above) answers what the pinned DuckDB answers for
#   each case of tests/subquery/cases.sql, on one and three workers: scalar
#   subqueries in a WHERE, a SELECT list, a grouped SELECT list, a HAVING,
#   under CASE, nested, over an empty table and as a LIMIT body with no row;
#   IN and NULL-aware NOT IN over one-table and whole bodies (DISTINCT, GROUP
#   BY / HAVING, a join, an expression), with and without NULL; the shapes it
#   does not serve are refused by name at their step; every case is run.
# - test_run_sql_joins: run_sql over six TPC-H-typed Parquet tables (INT32 and
#   INT64 keys with NULLs and unmatched keys, DECIMAL(15,2) money, DATE32): the
#   shapes of Q3, Q5 (a six-table join on two keys) and Q10 as comma joins in
#   WHERE, with a revenue sum by one column, and their count(*), equal an oracle
#   computed row by row in the test; JOIN ... ON counts the key pairs over
#   INT32, INT64 and mixed keys; the unedited Q3 text is a named LOWERING_
#   refusal at step lower.
# - test_run_sql_int_sum: run_sql over INT64 and INT32 Parquet columns in several
#   row groups: an integer sum() is declared DECIMAL(38, 0) and exact past the
#   INT64 range (MAX and MIN rows, partials that each pass INT64, {MAX, MAX, MIN,
#   MIN} = -2, per-group totals), an empty or all-NULL sum is NULL, and
#   expressions, CASE and HAVING over the sum run.
# - test_run_sql_spill: a GROUP BY of 60,001 groups over a memory budget its
#   tables outgrow, with a spill directory, answers an oracle computed in the
#   test (count, exact sum, min, max, NULLs, the NULL key) on one and two
#   workers, as the same query with no budget does, and leaves the directory
#   empty; the same budget without one is refused at execute; a query that
#   fits creates nothing; an empty spill directory option and one that cannot
#   be created are named refusals.
# - test_run_plan: run_plan (engine_plan) of a plan bound from SQL on one
#   context, run on a fresh context with no table registered: a filter with a
#   sort and an aggregate answer what run_sql answers, directly and through
#   plan_to_bytes and plan_from_bytes, and parse no statement on that context
#   (statements_parsed stays 0, while run_sql counts one per statement, a
#   refused one included); a plan over a file removed after binding is refused
#   at lower naming the file, and the context runs on.
# - test_run_sql_multifile: run_sql over a table of several Parquet files: a
#   directory (files at any depth; a dot-name and a non-Parquet file skipped), a
#   glob, a brace glob, read_parquet('<glob>') and FROM '<glob>' in SQL text; a
#   Hive tree whose int, string and date keys are columns after the data columns
#   in key order, filters on a key (files pruned) and on a data column, a key
#   alone, GROUP BY a key, count(*), ORDER BY and LIMIT; hive_partitioning =
#   false; a glob with no file, a directory in SQL text and files of different
#   schemas are named refusals at their step; a replaced file is read again.
# - test_run_sql_s3: run_sql_with over catalog tables whose source names an S3
#   bucket, read through S3ScanFs over the fake S3 (objectstore_s3, aws_core
#   and komira_test_fake_s3 are test_deps for it): a filter and a
#   projection, a GROUP BY and a count, a table of two keys, and one key in two
#   buckets under UNION ALL each answer what the local files answer; a missing
#   and a refused object are the store's NOT_FOUND and PERMISSION_DENIED at step
#   execute; run_sql refuses the S3 scan with SCAN_FS_UNSERVED, and so does
#   the S3 opener a gs:// table.
# - test_run_sql_gcs_azure: run_sql_with over catalog tables whose source
#   names a GCS bucket or an Azure container (the scheme from source_url's
#   reading of a gs:// or az:// URL), read through GcsScanFs over
#   objectstore_gcs's in-memory fake and through AzureScanFs over
#   komira_test_fake_azure's fake Blob service (objectstore_gcs, azure_blob,
#   azure_core, source_url and komira_test_fake_azure are test_deps for it):
#   a filter and a projection, a GROUP BY and a count, a table of two keys,
#   and one key in two buckets under UNION ALL each answer what the local
#   files answer; a missing and a refused object are the store's NOT_FOUND and
#   PERMISSION_DENIED at step execute; run_sql, and the opener of the other
#   store, refuse each with SCAN_FS_UNSERVED naming the store.
# - test_run_sql_no_from: SELECT 1 AS n is one INT32 row holding 1, as in
#   DuckDB; a result literal is INT32 from -2147483647 to 2147483647 and
#   INT64 otherwise, as DuckDB types it, while a literal a derived table holds
#   stays INT64 and a UNION ALL of literals is INT32; FROM-less expressions,
#   count(*) and a false WHERE run; a literal over a Parquet table; in-memory
#   sources of several batches and of none.
# - test_run_sql_union_all: UNION ALL over Parquet tables equals DuckDB:
#   INTEGER/BIGINT/FLOAT/DOUBLE/DECIMAL branches widened to DuckDB's common
#   type, three branches, a literal branch, aggregates, GROUP BY and WHERE over
#   a derived union, an empty branch, the first branch's names, LIMIT, and the
#   refusals of a union with no common type and of an ORDER BY before UNION ALL;
#   and an ORDER BY with no union (the sort sinks' data reaches the builder).
# - test_run_sql_copy_parquet: COPY ... TO a local Parquet file through
#   run_sql answers one BIGINT row `Count`; the file read back equals the query
#   (a table, a query, an empty result, 130,000 rows in two row groups, every
#   codec as the footer names it, a COPY over the file it reads, a relative
#   path); an unknown extension, a URL, CREATE TABLE AS and a TIMESTAMP
#   column are named refusals that touch no file; a missing
#   directory, a directory at the path and an injected full disk leave no
#   temporary file and the old file whole.
# - test_run_sql_copy_text: COPY ... TO a local CSV or newline-delimited JSON
#   file through run_sql writes the bytes DuckDB writes for the same rows
#   (NULLs, quoting, the empty string, dates, decimals, doubles), with the
#   format from the extension or FORMAT, gzip and zstd from the extension or
#   COMPRESSION, an empty result as DuckDB writes it; lz4, a TIMESTAMP column
#   and two columns of one name are named refusals that touch no file; a
#   full disk leaves the old file and no temporary file.
# - test_copy_text_encoding: the CSV and JSON encoders on batches of every
#   type they write give DuckDB's bytes: each type and NULL, doubles across
#   fixed and exponent notation, NaN and the infinities, FLOATs, dates
#   before year 1 and after 9999, exact decimals, quoting and escaping;
#   gzip and zstd decompress to their input; the writable types.
# - test_run_sql_csv: read_csv through run_sql answers what the pinned DuckDB
#   answers (the :csv_oracle action below) for each case of tests/csv/cases.sql:
#   typed columns and their inferred types, quoted cells, NULL-bearing cells, a
#   `|` dialect and a header with no row, each over the committed file and over
#   a gzip copy; every case is run; a missing file is refused at bind.
# - test_run_sql_topn: ORDER BY with LIMIT and OFFSET through run_sql over
#   Parquet tables in small row groups, on 1, 3 and 7 workers: each case of the
#   order_by_limit corpus with a LIMIT or an OFFSET (ties at the cut, NULLS
#   FIRST and LAST, both directions, NaN above inf, a 20000-row table, total
#   orders carrying k) answers the pinned DuckDB's rows in its order; the
#   comparison reports a reordered, changed, missing and added row; every
#   staged case is run or listed as refused (an OFFSET with no LIMIT is refused
#   at bind by name); a negative LIMIT is refused at parse.
# - test_run_sql_cte: WITH queries through run_sql answer what the pinned
#   DuckDB answers (the :cte_oracle action above) for each case of
#   tests/cte/cases.sql: a CTE read once (filtered, chained, unread, with NULL
#   counts, sorted, inside a derived table) and read more than once (a grouped
#   aggregation, the same under two filters, a distinct three times, a filtered
#   scan, a limit, a subquery reading a CTE twice, an ungrouped
#   aggregation); every case is run; a grouped aggregation read twice is
#   lowered to one aggregation segment both branches read, a subquery holding
#   a repeat written twice to one aggregation segment, and a filtered scan read
#   twice to two scans.
# - test_run_sql_window: run_sql over a Parquet table DuckDB wrote (the
#   :window_oracle action above) answers what the pinned DuckDB answers for
#   each case of tests/window/cases.sql: row_number, rank, dense_rank,
#   percent_rank, cume_dist, ntile, lag and lead (offsets, defaults, BIGINT,
#   DOUBLE and VARCHAR), running sums over the default RANGE frame and over a
#   unique key, running min, max, avg and count over tied keys, whole-partition sum, count, avg, min and max, first_value,
#   last_value and nth_value, no PARTITION BY, two partition keys, several
#   windows in one SELECT, a WHERE, an outer ORDER BY and no row, a CTE holding
#   a window read twice, a window over a CTE aggregation read twice (and over a
#   union of the reads) and a join with a window on its left side beside a CTE
#   read twice, with NULL
#   keys, tied order keys and the empty string, on one and three workers, each
#   column of the right type; every case is run, sum, avg, min and max over an
#   INTEGER among them; a DATE partition key, a DECIMAL order key and a float
#   default over a BIGINT lag are LOWERING_WINDOW_UNSERVED refusals at step
#   lower.
# - test_run_sql_window_frames: run_sql over two Parquet tables DuckDB wrote
#   (the :window_frames_oracle action above) answers what the pinned DuckDB
#   answers for each case of tests/window_frames/cases.sql, on one and three
#   workers: sliding, empty-at-the-edge, running and tail ROWS frames with
#   sum, count, avg, min, max, first_value, last_value and nth_value, a
#   descending order and offsets past the partition; RANGE frames with offsets
#   over a BIGINT key with ties and NULLs, an INTEGER key with an offset past
#   32 bits and a DOUBLE key with NaN, infinities, -0.0 and NULL; no PARTITION
#   BY, ranking and LAG ignoring their frame, a WHERE and no row; BIGINT and
#   INTEGER keys at the ends of their range; every case is run; a RANGE bound
#   that leaves the key's type fails at step execute with DuckDB's overflow
#   error, and a frame DuckDB refuses fails at step bind with its reason.
# - test_run_sql_set_ops: INTERSECT and EXCEPT through run_sql answer what the
#   pinned DuckDB answers (the :set_ops_oracle action above) for each case of
#   tests/set_ops/cases.sql, on one worker and on three: NULL rows equal, an
#   all-NULL and an empty input, repeated rows, several columns of mixed types,
#   one column of each key type, NaN, inputs widened to a common type, the
#   precedence of INTERSECT over UNION ALL and EXCEPT, a sort and an
#   aggregation over the result; every case is run; INTERSECT ALL and EXCEPT
#   ALL are refused at parse by name.
# - test_run_sql_timers_off: Q6 through run_sql on four workers in the default
#   build gives the oracle's answer and leaves every phase timer
#   (phase_timers) unregistered and at zero.
